// pf_site_in.vfl - PRIM wrangle.  B0's READ side.
//
// B0 is the SITE CONTRACT (§12.4) and it ships as an ADAPTER (§0.0a): it
// ingests today's PLANAR S8 lot - one closed polygon per lot - and stamps
// §12.4's volume + face-role schema in its degenerate planar form, where the
// role belongs to an EDGE and VERTEX is the only class that can hold one value
// per edge.  Streets needs no change, so no seam mismatch can arise and the
// work stays reversible.  ⚠️ The schema is Hannes' to ratify (§0.0g row 1) and
// nothing here records it as ratified.
//
// ⛔ WHY THE READ IS A SEPARATE NODE FROM THE WRITE - AND THE REASON THIS FILE
// USED TO GIVE WAS MEASURED FALSE.  It said "a wrangle that WRITES an
// attribute has already created it by the time `has*attrib` is asked in the
// same file".  Measured three ways on 22.0.398 by an independent audit, with a
// positive control: `hasvertexattrib(0, "pf_setback")` returns 0 inside a
// wrangle that writes `pf_setback` - asked BEFORE the write, asked AFTER it,
// and under the `f@pf_setback` binding form - while the control (the attribute
// genuinely present on the input) returns 1.  So a single node COULD have
// tested the input.  ⚠️ The split is kept anyway and nothing here depends on
// it: everything written by this file lands on `_*` scratch, so every test
// below is asked of the INPUT and of nothing this file has touched, and that
// property is checkable by reading the file rather than by trusting a claim
// about the compiler.  The disproved sentence was B0's headline structural
// argument and it stood in six places; this is one of them.
//
// WHAT THIS CANNOT SEE: whether the roles it was handed are the RIGHT roles.
// A role comes from which lot line touches a street (§10a) and is not
// derivable from geometry, so a lot that arrives untagged gets "" here - which
// resolves to the template's `defaultSetbackM` through the cascade - and never
// a guess at `front`.  Nor can it see a lot whose points are SHARED with
// another prim; like `pf_inset.vfl` it assumes one prim per point.

int pts[] = primpoints(0, @primnum);
int n = len(pts);

// §12.4: `pf_site_id` is DETAIL on a single-site stream and PRIM once a stream
// carries several, "and B0 owns that conversion".  Both forms in, prim out,
// because prim is what B2 cooks.
//
// ⛔ A LOT THAT CARRIES NEITHER DOES NOT GET ITS PRIMITIVE NUMBER, AND THE
// FIRST BUILD SAID IT DID.  §12.7 forbids deriving an id from generation
// order, and the primitive number IS generation order: measured, the same two
// unidentified lots cooked in the opposite order swap ids, and every
// downstream `<site>:B2:v<k>:<slot>` address moves with them - which
// `elem_ids_structural` is structurally unable to see, because it compares the
// id SET and the set is identical.
// ⚠️ AND THAT IS THE NORMAL PATH RATHER THAN AN EDGE CASE.  The first build
// also claimed "every lot streets produces carries one".  It does not:
// `tests/citygen/checks.py`'s lot allowlist publishes `block_id` / `lot_id`
// and NOTHING in this repo writes `pf_site_id`, so until B0 is taught to read
// the streets lot identity by its own name - which needs that attribute's
// STORAGE settled with the streets owner (D223) and is the named next step -
// every building built from a real S8 lot reaches this line.
// What an unidentified lot gets instead is its own PLAN POSITION, quantised to
// the centimetre and hashed: deterministic, independent of the order the prims
// arrive in, and distinct for lots that do not sit on top of one another.
// ⚠️ WHAT IT CANNOT DO: survive the lot MOVING, which is the exposure §0.0a
// already records against `elem_id` upstream, and it is not this fallback's to
// fix.  What it fixes is generation order, which is the one thing §12.7 names.
//
// ⛔ AND ITS RESIDUAL IS A COLLISION CEILING, NOT "TWO LOTS SHARING A
// CENTROID".  That is what this file used to say, and two non-overlapping lots
// cannot share a plan centroid to the centimetre - so the stated residual was
// one that cannot happen while the one that can went unstated.  The id lives in
// 31 bits (`& 2147483647`, the sign bit masked off so an id is never negative),
// so the birthday point is ~46 000 lots.  Measured on a Python model of this
// arithmetic: 0 collisions on a realistic 9 600-lot 20 x 30 m grid, 0 at 46 225
// lots on a 5 m grid, and 18 at 160 000 lots on a 5 m grid - and they arrive in
// STRUCTURED PAIRS, because `h(x1)^h(z1) == h(x2)^h(z2)` forces
// `h(x1)^h(z2) == h(x2)^h(z1)`, an aliasing property of XOR over a lattice, and
// city lots are on a lattice.  A collision gives two parcels the same
// `pf_site_id`, hence identical `pf_elem_id` addresses, hence one lot's
// overrides on another - SILENTLY, because nothing in production detects it
// (`site_contract/identity`'s duplicate-prim term catches it in the test only).
// ⚠️ AND THE CEILING IS RECORDED RATHER THAN RAISED, deliberately.  The id is
// an INT attribute, so 31 bits is one bit off everything there is; and the
// pairing is inherent to any `f(x) op g(z)` scheme - a bijective avalanche
// after the XOR cannot remove it, because it maps equal inputs to equal
// outputs.  Escaping it means a wider id, which is a §12.4 SCHEMA decision and
// Hannes' (§0.0g row 1), or - the step already named - reading the streets
// `lot_id` by name and never minting at all.
//
// ⚠️ EVERY READ BELOW IS ASSIGNED TO A TYPED LOCAL FIRST.  `prim()`, `vertex()`
// and `detail()` return an untyped value, so passing one straight into
// `setvertexattrib()` - or into a ternary - is ambiguous and the compiler says
// so (dev-loop trap list; it cost a cook here before it cost a comment).
vector centroid = set(0.0, 0.0, 0.0);
for (int k = 0; k < n; k++) centroid += point(0, "P", pts[k]);
centroid /= max(float(n), 1.0);
int site = ((int(rint(centroid.x * 100.0)) * 73856093)
            ^ (int(rint(centroid.z * 100.0)) * 19349663)) & 2147483647;
if (hasprimattrib(0, "pf_site_id")) {
    int v = prim(0, "pf_site_id", @primnum);
    site = v;
} else if (hasdetailattrib(0, "pf_site_id")) {
    int v = detail(0, "pf_site_id");
    site = v;
}
i@_site = site;

// §12.4: the seed belongs to the SITE - "same seed + template + overrides =>
// identical geometry".  A lot arriving with one keeps it.  `R4-6`: a lot
// arriving WITHOUT one used to reach `stamp()`'s un-seeded branch, where a
// template key no template defines gave every site in the stream the same
// seed, 0, so §12.4's per-site determinism row was silently unimplemented
// rather than loudly missing.  The site id is deterministic, distinct per site
// and structural, so it is what an unseeded lot gets.
i@_hasseed = hasprimattrib(0, "pf_seed");
int seed = site;
if (i@_hasseed) {
    int v = prim(0, "pf_seed", @primnum);
    seed = v;
}
i@_seed = seed;

// §12.4: `pf_style_template` is prim/detail and "resolvable through the
// cascade (zone default -> region -> per-site)".  The node's own parameter is
// cascade level 2 and loses to both.
string st = "";
if (hasprimattrib(0, "pf_style_template")) {
    string v = prim(0, "pf_style_template", @primnum);
    st = v;
} else if (hasdetailattrib(0, "pf_style_template")) {
    string v = detail(0, "pf_style_template");
    st = v;
}
s@_style = (st != "") ? st : chs("style");

// The two per-EDGE fields.  §12.4 declares BOTH classes legal for each: prim
// on the volume form, vertex on the planar one.
//
// The ROLE is normalised here, to VERTEX, because it is STRUCTURAL - it says
// what the site IS, so it belongs to the site contract, and `pf_mass.vfl`
// reads exactly one class.  The SETBACK is deliberately NOT normalised here:
// it is a cascade level-5 OVERRIDE, the cascade is `stamp()`'s job, and
// `stamp()` reads both classes for `R4-3`.  One mechanism per contract; the
// prim `pf_setback` (if any) passes through this node untouched and is swept
// at B2's output by `CLEAN`, which is the other half of `R4-3`.
//
// ⚠️ An absent vertex `pf_setback` is written as the SENTINEL, never as 0 -
// and a vertex sentinel is what lets `stamp()` fall through to a prim-class
// authoring.  Writing 0 here would silently answer a question the artist had
// asked of a different class.
int hasvs = hasvertexattrib(0, "pf_setback");
int hasvr = hasvertexattrib(0, "pf_face_role");
string pr = "";
if (hasprimattrib(0, "pf_face_role")) {
    string v = prim(0, "pf_face_role", @primnum);
    pr = v;
}

for (int k = 0; k < n; k++) {
    int vi = vertexindex(0, @primnum, k);
    float sb = -1.0;
    if (hasvs) { float v = vertex(0, "pf_setback", vi); sb = v; }
    string rl = pr;
    if (hasvr) { string v = vertex(0, "pf_face_role", vi); rl = v; }
    setvertexattrib(0, "_setback", @primnum, k, sb);
    setvertexattrib(0, "_role", @primnum, k, rl);
}
